    {
      "directory": "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962",
      "id": "q-59760",
      "status": "supported",
      "reason": null,
      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-59760",
        "knowledge_point_id": "88580aff-e043-58ea-a8ca-1ce32026428f",
        "knowledge_point": "Constructing the Speed-Time Graph for a Real-Life Scenario",
        "problem": "A particle accelerates from rest with a constant acceleration of $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$ for the first $4$ hours of its motion. It then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. Which graph represents the described motion?",
        "worked_solution": "We start the first line from the point $(0,0),$ since the particle starts from rest. Then, because the acceleration is $5\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2},$ we draw a line with slope $5.$ \n\nHowever, because the particle is only accelerating for the first $4$ hours, we stop the line at $t=4.$ At this time, the particle's speed has increased by $4\\cdot 5=20\\text{ km/h}.$ So, the first line segment goes from $(0,0)$ to $(4,20).$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/cf0471f38486963f4237b4fab7c1cd109d02cc2d7ac3e30990831654f04c5b11.png)\n\nThe particle then travels at a constant speed for the next $6$ hours. So, the speed remains at $20\\,\\text{km/h}$ for the next $6$ hours, until $t=4+6=10.$\n\nSo, the second line segment stops at the point $(10,20).$ The final graph is\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13945962/assets/fae4c7d8cfc79dd70041ebd697226408a5cdd0676ad2ddfaad1500ea1ba0a76e.png)",
        "answer_fields": [
