      "question": {
        "math_academy_id": "q-76773",
        "knowledge_point_id": "fe83dedc-3034-5e3a-b546-dd57c0ccc6e8",
        "knowledge_point": "Finding the Area of a Triangle by Solving for a Variable Given an Angle",
        "problem": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13946119/assets/ca9c6640b6cb239ae19b195731d8f605adae8826f66e10b075f7b0ad4a60690f.png)\n\nFind the area (in square units) of $△ABC$ shown above.",
        "worked_solution": "In this case, the area of the right triangle is\n\n$$\nA=\\frac{1}{2}AB\\cdot BC.\n$$\n\nWe are given that $m∠A={30}^{∘}.$ Notice that relative to $∠A,$ we have expressions for the lengths of the opposite side $\\overset{―}{BC}$ and adjacent side $\\overset{―}{AB}.$ Hence, we can use the tangent ratio to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{tan}⁡(m∠A) & =\\frac{BC}{AB} \\\\ \\operatorname{tan}⁡({30}^{∘}) & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+4} \\\\ \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3} & =\\frac{\\sqrt{3}\\,x}{x+4} \\\\ \\sqrt{3}(x+4) & =3\\cdot \\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ \\sqrt{3}\\,x+4\\sqrt{3} & =3\\sqrt{3}\\,x \\\\ -2\\sqrt{3}\\,x & =-4\\sqrt{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{4\\sqrt{3}}{2\\sqrt{3}} \\\\ x & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the side lengths are \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}BC & =\\sqrt{3}\\,x=\\sqrt{3}(2)=2\\sqrt{3}, \\\\ AB & =x+4=2+4=6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the area of $△ABC$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot AB\\cdot BC \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot 6\\cdot 2\\sqrt{3} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
