        "knowledge_point": "Solving a System Containing Decimals in Both Equations",
        "problem": "Solve the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.3x-0.1y=0.6 \\\\ 0.6x+0.8y=0.2\\end{aligned}\n$$",
        "worked_solution": "To get rid of the decimals, we multiply both equations by $10,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}0.3x-0.1y=0.6 & |\\,\\times 10 \\\\ 0.6x+0.8y=0.2 & |\\,\\times 10\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 \\\\ 6x+8y=2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that if we divide the second equation by $(-2),$ we will get a $-3x$ term in the second equation that can cancel with the $3x$ term in the first equation:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 &  \\\\ 6x+8y=2 & |\\,÷(-2)\\end{aligned}\\,⟹\\,{\\begin{aligned}3x-y=6 \\\\ -3x-4y=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAdding the two equations eliminates the $x$-variable and allows us to solve for $y:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\cancel{3x}-y & =6 \\\\ -\\cancel{3x}-4y & =-1 \\\\ (-y-4y) & =(6-1) \\\\ -5y & =5 \\\\ y & =-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can substitute $y=-1$ into the equation $3x-y=6$ to solve for $x:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-y & =6 \\\\ 3x-(-1) & =6 \\\\ 3x & =5 \\\\ x & =\\frac{5}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the solution to the system of equations is $x=\\frac{5}{3}$ and $y=-1.$",
